_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 33
---
order: 33
---
short_label: VI.33
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: Angles in equal circles have the same ratio as the circumferences on which they stand whether they stand at the centers or at the circumferences.
----
proof:

Let {ABC|ABCc} and {DEF|DEFc} be equal circles, and let the angles {BGC|angBGC} and {EHF|angEHF} be angles at their centers {G} and {H}, and the angles {BAC|angBAC} and {EDF|angEDF} angles at the circumferences.

I say that the circumference {BC} is to the circumference {EF} as the angle {BGC|angBGC} is to the angle {EHF|angEHF}, and as the angle {BAC|angBAC} is to the angle {EDF|angEDF}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="500" height="300" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="VI.33 diagram" src="propVI33.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce's figure draws the equal circles ABC and DEF, the angles BAC and
    // EDF as joined lines, the central angles as filled sectors GBC and HEF,
    // and the consecutive equal sectors GCK, GKL and HFM, HMN with their
    // radii GK, GL, HM, HN. K, L, M, N and that construction are withheld
    // until the proof makes them.
    //
    // The two circles are 4-field elements as converted (an opaque
    // near-white face), so they are never highlighted: a highlighted element
    // is raised (#140) and the disc would cover the sectors. The faceless
    // twins ABCc / DEFc carry the name, reached as {ABC|ABCc}. Everything
    // else added is transparent (;0;0;0;0) or an angle marker (hidden at
    // rest): the circumferences BC, CK, KL, BL and EF, FM, MN, EN as
    // faceless sectors (the III.26 convention), and markers for the angles,
    // since the filled sectors would be raised over the joins.
    //
    // Markers: BGC = CGK = KGL share a colour, as do EHF = FHM = MHN; BGL and
    // EHN are the multiples. None is right.
    //
    // Draggables (checked against the bundle): A, B, C, H free and the
    // circleSliders D, E, F — the circles and angles of slide 3. G and
    // K, L, M, N are derived and listed per slide.
