              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angBGL", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "For the same reason, whatever multiple the circumference {NE} is of {EF},",
              visible: HEQ,
              highlighted: ["EN","EF","angEHF","angFHM","angMHN"],
              transition: { mode: "parallel", animations: [
                  { elem: "angFHM", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "angMHN", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "the angle {NHE|angEHN} is also that multiple of the angle {EHF|angEHF}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["angEHN","angEHF"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angEHN", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "If the circumference {BL} equals the circumference {EN}, then the angle {BGL|angBGL} also equals the angle {EHN|angEHN};",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["BL","EN","angBGL","angEHN"] },
            { text: "if the circumference {BL} is greater than the circumference {EN}, then the angle {BGL|angBGL} is also greater than the angle {EHN|angEHN};",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["BL","EN","angBGL","angEHN"] },
            { text: "and, if less, less.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["BL","EN","angBGL","angEHN"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "circumferences and angles equal, greater, or less together", ref: "III.27" }] },
            { text: "There being then four magnitudes, two circumferences {BC} and {EF}, and two angles {BGC|angBGC} and {EHF|angEHF},",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["BC","EF","angBGC","angEHF"] },
            { text: "there have been taken, of the circumference {BC} and the angle {BGC|angBGC} equimultiples,",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["BC","angBGC"] },
            { text: "namely the circumference {BL} and the angle {BGL|angBGL},",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["BL","angBGL"] },
            { text: "and of the circumference {EF} and the angle {EHF|angEHF} equimultiples, namely the circumference {EN} and the angle {EHN|angEHN}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["EF","angEHF","EN","angEHN"] },
            { text: "And it has been proved that, if the circumference {BL} is in excess of the circumference {EN},",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["BL","EN"] },
            { text: "the angle {BGL|angBGL} is also in excess of the angle {EHN|angEHN}; if equal, equal; and if less, less.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["angBGL","angEHN"] },
            { text: "Therefore the circumference {BC} is to {EF} as the angle {BGC|angBGC} is to the angle {EHF|angEHF}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["BC","EF","angBGC","angEHF"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "BC : EF = angle BGC : angle EHF", ref: "V.Def.5" }] },
            { text: "But the angle {BGC|angBGC} is to the angle {EHF|angEHF} as the angle {BAC|angBAC} is to the angle {EDF|angEDF},",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["angBGC","angEHF","angBAC","angEDF"] },
            { text: "for they are doubles respectively.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["angBGC","angEHF","angBAC","angEDF"],
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "angle at the center double the angle at the circumference", ref: "III.20" },
                  { claim: "parts have the same ratio as their equimultiples", ref: "V.15" } ] },
            { text: "Therefore also the circumference {BC} is to the circumference {EF} as the angle {BGC|angBGC} is to the angle {EHF|angEHF},",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["BC","EF","angBGC","angEHF"] },
            { text: "and the angle {BAC|angBAC} to the angle {EDF|angEDF}.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["angBAC","angEDF"] },
            { text: "Therefore, angles in equal circles have the same ratio as the circumferences on which they stand",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["ABCc","DEFc","BC","EF"] },
            { text: "whether they stand at the centers or at the circumferences.",
              visible: ALL,
              highlighted: ["angBGC","angEHF","angBAC","angEDF"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "VI.33") return "#prop";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.Post.1": "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post1/",
        "III.20":   "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII20/",
        "III.27":   "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII27/",
        "V.15":     "/elements/books/bookV/propositions/propV15/",
        "V.Def.5":  "/elements/books/bookV/definitions/defV5/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

Make any number of consecutive circumferences {CK} and {KL} equal to the circumference {BC}, and any number of consecutive circumferences {FM}, {MN} equal to the circumference {EF}, and join {GK} and {GL} and {HM} and {HN}.

Then, since the circumferences {BC}, {CK}, and {KL} equal one another, the angles {BGC|angBGC}, {CGK|angCGK}, and {KGL|angKGL} also equal one another.  Therefore, whatever multiple the circumference {BL} is of {BC}, the angle {BGL|angBGL} is also that multiple of the angle {BGC|angBGC}. [!just III.27]

For the same reason, whatever multiple the circumference {NE} is of {EF}, the angle {NHE|angEHN} is also that multiple of the angle {EHF|angEHF}.

If the circumference {BL} equals the circumference {EN}, then the angle {BGL|angBGL} also equals the angle {EHN|angEHN};  if the circumference {BL} is greater than the circumference {EN}, then the angle {BGL|angBGL} is also greater than the angle {EHN|angEHN}; and, if less, less. [!just III.27]

There being then four magnitudes, two circumferences {BC} and {EF}, and two angles {BGC|angBGC} and {EHF|angEHF}, there have been taken, of the circumference {BC} and the angle {BGC|angBGC} equimultiples, namely the circumference {BL} and the angle {BGL|angBGL}, and of the circumference {EF} and the angle {EHF|angEHF} equimultiples, namely the circumference {EN} and the angle {EHN|angEHN}.

And it has been proved that, if the circumference {BL} is in excess of the circumference {EN}, the angle {BGL|angBGL} is also in excess of the angle {EHN|angEHN}; if equal, equal; and if less, less.

Therefore the circumference {BC} is to {EF} as the angle {BGC|angBGC} is to the angle {EHF|angEHF}. [!just V.Def.5]

But the angle {BGC|angBGC} is to the angle {EHF|angEHF} as the angle {BAC|angBAC} is to the angle {EDF|angEDF}, for they are doubles respectively. [!just V.15, III.20]

Therefore also the circumference {BC} is to the circumference {EF} as the angle {BGC|angBGC} is to the angle {EHF|angEHF}, and the angle {BAC|angBAC} to the angle {EDF|angEDF}.

Therefore, *angles in equal circles have the same ratio as the circumferences on which they stand whether they stand at the centers or at the circumferences.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

This proposition stands apart from the rest in this book since it depends on none of them.
It is, however, analogous to the first proposition @VI.1 of the book. Whereas VI.1 established a proportion between lines and plane figures, this proposition establishes a proportion between angles and portions of circumferences cut off by those angles.

In the *Elements* Euclid restricted his study of lengths of arcs to circles of the same radius.  He did not compare arcs of different sized circles.  Later, however, Archimedes did just that in his *Measurement of a Circle*.

This proposition is used in three consecutive propositions in Book XIII starting with @XIII.8 to convert statements about arcs to statements about angles.  Incidentally, all three have to do with regular pentagons inscribed in circles.
